            'eligibility_html' => '<p>MFAI material states that all applicants for visas and permits must have an acceptable standard of health for the intended length of stay — specifically, an applicant must be unlikely to pose a danger to public health and unlikely to impose costs or demands on publicly funded health services. This is a notable point for anyone travelling specifically for treatment, since it is framed as an admission bar rather than an accommodation, and its practical application to inbound medical-treatment travellers was not independently confirmed from available sources.</p><p>For stays of 6 months or more, a medical clearance letter from the Ministry of Health is required, based on an examination completed offshore before travel, using the Ministry\'s list of approved panel physicians (referencing Immigration New Zealand\'s panel). No distinct provision for an accompanying attendant was found — each traveller, including any attendant, is expected to separately meet the same entry and health-standard conditions.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Its Own Immigration System, Not Mainland New Zealand\'s</h4><p>The Cook Islands runs immigration under its own Immigration Act 2021 via MFAI — it is a realm of New Zealand, but does not use mainland New Zealand visa categories, and Indian citizens should not assume mainland NZ visa rules apply here.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>No Separate Medical Visa, and a Health Standard to Note</h4><p>Short medical visits appear to fall under the ordinary automatic Visitor Permit. However, all applicants must meet a general health standard that includes not imposing costs on publicly funded health services — a point worth clarifying with MFAI for anyone travelling specifically to receive treatment there.</p><p>For stays of 6 months or longer, a medical clearance letter obtained before travel is required. Our consultants can help clarify which permit class and health-clearance process applies to your specific treatment plan.</p>',
